1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Added on Tuesday, May 12, 2020:'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Not much information is known about Young Ki Chung.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was most likely from 廟邊鄉 (Miào biān xiāng), otherwise known as Miaobian Township. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Information from Mark Eugene Chung:'''
2 CONT
2 CONT YOUNG KI CHUNG was born c. 1876 in China.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Notes for YOUNG KI CHUNG:'''
2 CONT
2 CONT The Chung line of your family has been traced back to Young Ki Chung, who would have been born sometime before about 1876 if he was 21 when his son was born. Of course, he may have been much older or even a little younger. Young's
2 CONC  name was taken from the marriage certificate his son, Young Tat Chung, a document that also tells us Young Ki Chung had died before the marriage took place. His occupation was unknown, which suggests that he was not in close cont
2 CONC act with his son.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is not known whether Young Ki ever left China, his homeland, nor who he married or the names of any of his other children. We have a few possible sightings of him on various documents, but no way of being sure that we have foun
2 CONC d the right man. The most likely reference we found is on the 1911 census, when a [Yung Cheung], who was married and age 34, was working as a fireman (stoker) onboard the ''SS Grainton'' off Newport. This man had been born in Cant
2 CONC on, China. The ship belonged to the Ocean Steam Ship Company and was launched on 26 March 1911, only a few weeks before the census was taken. Sadly, it sank on 31 May 1928 off the Caribbean island of Saba.
2 CONT
2 CONT Other possible sightings of Young Ki were in Hawaii and San Francisco, however, without more information about your ancestor it is unlikely we will ever make any firm connections to these men.
