1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Duke_De_of_Qin
2 CONT
2 CONT Duke De of Qin (Chinese: 秦德公; pinyin: Qín Dé Gōng; 710–676 BC), personal name unknown, was a duke of the state of Qin during the Eastern Zhou dynasty who reigned from 677 to 676 BC.[1][2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Duke De was the second of the three sons of his father Duke Xian. His younger half-brother Chuzi I was the first to succeed Duke Xian in 704 BC, but was killed six years later. His older brother Duke Wu then ascended the throne an
2 CONC d reigned for 20 years. Although Duke Wu had a son named Bai (白), when he died in 678 BC it was Duke De who succeeded him, while Prince Bai was enfeoffed at the capital Pingyang.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT In the first year of his reign, Duke De moved the capital to Yong (in present-day Fengxiang, Shaanxi), which would remain the capital of Qin until almost three centuries later, when Duke Xian (Shixi) moved the capital to Yueyang i
2 CONC n 383 BC.[2] However, Duke De reigned for only two years before dying in 676 BC at the age of 34. He had three sons, who would in turn ascend the throne as Duke Xuan, Duke Cheng, and Duke Mu, respectively.[1][2]
